The patch introduces scheduler starvation for edge jobs behind deferred node work in no-embedder sessions, and the late-enrolment write path is not crash-atomic despite depending on that property. These can break drain/readiness semantics.

Full review comments:

- [P1] Don't filter no-embedder jobs after the limited scan — /home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/orch-0.8.20-s20c/src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/src/lib.rs:12412-12412
  When a no-embedder session has more than `PROJECTION_SCAN_FETCH` pending node rows before a pending edge body, `next_pending_projection_jobs` returns only those earlier node jobs due to its ordered `LIMIT`, and this post-fetch filter drops all of them. The dispatcher then sets `pending_scan` false without ever seeing the later edge job, so the edge body that this comment says should still terminate is left unscheduled until another wake, and `drain` can time out indefinitely for that edge workload.

- [P2] Make late enrolment atomic with un-stranding — /home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/orch-0.8.20-s20c/src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/src/lib.rs:4902-4902
  For the late-enrolment path, this `INSERT` autocommits before `unstrand_after_late_enrolment` opens its `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, so a crash in between can leave the kind registered while the older rows still have `'up_to_date'` terminals and no vectors. On the next open, `kind_is_vector_indexed` is already true, so future writes skip the un-stranding path and readiness can report ready for those stranded rows until the user manually re-applies the projection; the registry insert and terminal/cursor repair need to commit as one transaction.
The patch introduces scheduler starvation for edge jobs behind deferred node work in no-embedder sessions, and the late-enrolment write path is not crash-atomic despite depending on that property. These can break drain/readiness semantics.

